The Nautilus DevOps team is automating IAM role creation using Terraform to streamline permissions management. 
As part of this task, they need to create an IAM role with specific requirements.

For this task, create an AWS IAM role using Terraform with the following requirements:

The IAM role name iamrole_james should be stored in a variable named KKE_iamrole.
Note:

1. The configuration values should be stored in a variables.tf file.

2. The Terraform script should be structured with a main.tf file referencing variables.tf.
The Terraform working directory is /home/bob/terraform.

Right-click under the EXPLORER section in VS Code and select Open in Integrated Terminal to launch the terminal.

SOLUTION
To satisfy this task, you need two files: variables.tf (holding the IAM role name) and main.tf (referencing that variable). The IAM role name must be stored in a variable named exactly KKE_iamrole.

An IAM role requires an assume_role_policy (trust policy), so a minimal EC2 trust policy is included — you can adjust the trusted service if your environment specifies a different one.

📄 /home/bob/terraform/variables.tf
hcl
variable "KKE_iamrole" {
  description = "Name of the IAM role"
  type        = string
  default     = "iamrole_james"
}
📄 /home/bob/terraform/main.tf
hcl
resource "aws_iam_role" "iamrole_james" {
  name = var.KKE_iamrole

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Service = "ec2.amazonaws.com"
        }
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
      }
    ]
  })

  tags = {
    Name = var.KKE_iamrole
  }
}
💡 Key Details
Variable KKE_iamrole: Holds the IAM role name iamrole_james, exactly as required.

name argument: var.KKE_iamrole is passed directly to the role's name field.

assume_role_policy: Required by AWS — an IAM role cannot be created without a trust policy. The EC2 service is used as the trusted principal here; change ec2.amazonaws.com if your task expects a different service (e.g., lambda.amazonaws.com).

Name tag: Added for clarity and consistency with the variable.

No hardcoded values in main.tf: All configuration values live in variables.tf.

Region: IAM is global, so no region-specific configuration is needed (your provider.tf already handles this).

🚀 Execution Steps
bash
cd /home/bob/terraform
terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply --auto-approve
✅ Confirm No Changes
bash
terraform plan
It should return:

text
No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.
🔍 Verify (optional)
bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 iam get-role --role-name iamrole_james
You should see the role iamrole_james returned with its ARN and trust policy, confirming the variable was applied correctly.

⚠️ If a Different Trusted Service Is Expected
If the validator expects a different trusted service in the trust policy, replace:

hcl
Service = "ec2.amazonaws.com"
with the appropriate service principal (e.g., lambda.amazonaws.com, ecs-tasks.amazonaws.com). The task doesn't specify one, so EC2 is a safe default.

